# Where the @fmmenchi packages come from.
#
# They are published to GitHub Packages, not npmjs — see shared-platform's
# `publishConfig`. The mapping is SCOPED on purpose: a bare `registry=` line here would
# send every package in the workspace to GitHub Packages, which serves none of them.
#
# The TOKEN is deliberately not here. GitHub Packages wants one even for public packages,
# but pnpm refuses to expand environment variables in credentials read from a project
# .npmrc — this file is committed, and expanding a secret into it could hand that secret
# to a registry someone else controls. It has to come from a source pnpm trusts:
#
#   in CI      the `setup` brick takes it as `github-token`, from the per-run
#              secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN — nothing to create or rotate. It is passed together
#              with `registry-url`, and that pairing is not belt-and-braces: the token
#              arrives as NODE_AUTH_TOKEN, and the auth line that would resolve it cannot
#              live HERE. pnpm ignores it, and says why — "environment variables are not
#              expanded in registry credentials that come from a project .npmrc". The
#              registry-url makes actions/setup-node write that line into a USER-level
#              npmrc instead, which pnpm does expand.
#   locally    put it in your own ~/.npmrc, once per machine:
#                pnpm config set "//npm.pkg.github.com/:_authToken" <a PAT with read:packages>
#
# Without it, requests to this registry go out unauthenticated and fail — but only when
# something actually resolves here.
@fmmenchi:registry=https://npm.pkg.github.com
